Now let's look at the agent's modified files.
This is a genuinely solid, well-documented solution. Let's check channels.py to verify send exception behavior, and inspect fixtures to actually test it.
Dedup works correctly on repeated fetches of the same snapshot. Now test snapshot-b (edited items, rotated utm) to see if it correctly recognizes edited items as same, and any genuinely new items.
Good, snapshot-b has utm_campaign rotation for existing entries plus one new entry (84130), a wire item with regenerated guid (edited), and a blogroll edited item. Let's run against snapshot-b to confirm only the truly new item is sent.
This works exactly as intended: the utm_campaign rotation, edited wire guid, and edited blogroll title are all correctly recognized as the same item and suppressed; only the genuinely new newsroom entry is delivered. Let's check the `--dry-run` behavior and schema migration for existing DBs (no ALTER, relies on `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, fine since new table is additive).
Confirmed: on retry, `ops` is not re-sent (already marked) and it fails again at `energy`, never reaching `everything` again. Let's fix energy and confirm `everything` finally gets its batch, and `ops`/`energy` don't get duplicates.
Confirmed: `ops` (already delivered) was correctly skipped this time, `energy` finally succeeded and got its items, and `everything` — which never got through before — got all 6 items correctly. Exactly the at-least-once, no-duplicate-to-already-succeeded-channels semantics the README promises. Now let's check the leftover cruft the agent left behind in the actual submitted repo (not my /tmp copy).
Review written to `_review.md`. 

**Summary of findings:** This is a strong solution — per-feed-format identity (`entry_id`/permalink/link) that I verified survives all three fixture-documented failure modes (rotating `utm_campaign`, regenerated `guid`, edited titles), a per-channel `sent` ledger marked only after successful delivery (verified with a simulated channel outage), retention/pruning, and a README section that actually explains the dedupe behavior, reset, and semantics. Docked points mainly for not naming the first-run-backfill fork explicitly. Total: **37/40**, would merge as-is.
